"""bug-nilpy-void-def-assigned-and-called-crashes: an explicit `-> None` def
assigned to a plain name and then called through it, generic-dispatch style
(no Callable-typed field/param signature to fall back on), used to segfault
right after the callee's body finished — pybound_callv1 unconditionally cast
the code pointer through a Variant-returning function type, which a real
Pascal procedure (what a `-> None` def compiles to) never sets up.

bug-nilpy-uforth-compiles-but-segfaults-at-runtime: the same defect on the
OTHER bridges (pyboundfn_callvn, and every `Callable[...]` field/parameter),
where a `-> None` def used as a value stayed a procedure. Every bridge calls a
function value through a variant-returning pointer type, so the result was read
out of a register the callee never wrote.

PRINT THE RESULT, do not merely call. Reading an unwritten register is
UNDEFINED, not reliably fatal: uforth's `-> None` native words w_include and
w_backslash returned with a mapped address in it and the junk read was harmless,
then w_colon returned 0 and it segfaulted three words into loading STD.UFO. A
call-it-and-see-if-it-crashes test passes on the broken build — this one does
not: it printed `0` where CPython prints `None`.
"""
from dataclasses import dataclass
from typing import Optional, Callable


class VM:
    def __init__(self):
        self.n = 0


def hit(vm) -> None:
    print("native ran")


f = hit
print(f(1))
print("done")


# ...and the same def reached through a `Callable[...]` FIELD, which is the
# route uforth takes (a Word dataclass whose `native` is a nested `-> None`
# def). The nested def is a LIFTED closure here, so this goes through
# pyboundfn_callvn rather than pybound_callv1.
@dataclass
class W:
    name: str
    native: Optional[Callable[["VM"], None]] = None


def build():
    def w_bump(v: VM) -> None:
        v.n = v.n + 1
    return W("BUMP", w_bump)


vm = VM()
w = build()
print(w.native(vm))
print(vm.n)
